d the  author as for chess peter started  playing the game when he was six and he  would become one of the strongest junior  competitors in fact he was so good that  he was awarded the title of life master  chess wasn't peter's only strong suit  though he was particularly talented in  mathematics and he would graduate as  valedictorian after high school peter  would attend stanford where he majored  in philosophy and though he enjoyed the  content he wasn't a fan of the liberal  biases embedded within education this  was one of peter's first entanglements  with controversy as he would turn around  and co-found the stanford review which  was a conservative libertarian newspaper  peter served as the editor-in-chief up  until graduation but this wasn't the end  of his support he still donates to the  newspaper to this day and he commonly  hires students from the newspaper  anyway after his undergraduate days  peter would turn around and attend  stanford once again to get his juris  doctor degree and yes this means that  peter was formally trained to be a  lawyer after graduation in 1992 he  started clerking for a judge in the us  appeals court and he would eventually  work out to be a securities lawyer in  new york but peter would end up quitting  this job and basically law as a whole  just seven months later stating that he  found little transcendental value in  this work  peter bounced around a little after  leaving law shortly working as a  derivatives trader and as a speech  writer but in 1996 he decided to return  back to california and look for a more  meaningful job he never found this  perfect job but he would find the  perfect career  peter didn't just jump straight into  paypal in fact he wasn't even looking to  start his own business he was however  determined to get involved in an  internet company as he saw the dotcom  boom unfold so he became a venture  capitalist in 1996 peter started theo  capital management and funded it using  money from friends family and his  personal savings peter's first  investment was one hundred thousand  dollars into a man named luke nosek luke  had the idea of creating a web-based  calendar but this idea didn't quite pan  out peter's second investment was also  in luke nozek but this time max levchin  was also involved and the idea was to  create a digital cryptography company  called confinity the company originally  sold security software for handheld  devices but they found a little success  in this area so peter decided to shift  the company into fintech with the launch  of something called paypal  today paypal has basically become  ubiquitous with online payments but this  wasn't peter's intention  you see peter's goal with paypal was  much more philosophical and intellectual  peter wanted to create a sort of  decentralized rebellion against  inflation similar to bitcoin peter hated  that the government was inflating away  currency and he thought that the  solution was creating a digital exchange  that allowed people to easily switch  between currencies this way paypal users  could basically boycott certain  currencies and force governments to  respond in 1999 peter said quote it will  be nearly impossible for corrupt  governments to steal wealth from their  people through their old means because  if they try the people will switch to  dollars or pounds or yen in effect  dumping the worthless local currency for  something more secure it looks to me  like peter was begging to be on the  government's watch list but you know  this was just peter being peter  unfortunately though the general public  didn't respond as peter expected and  paypal ended up as just a popular mode  of payment despite their popularity  running the business wasn't easy given  how much cash they were bleeding so in  the early 2000s paypal would merge with  a competitor called x.com and this is  where elon would get involved the merger  helped secure paypal's market share but  their financial situation didn't get any  better so the company was forced to go  public and they would soon be sold to  ebay while this made peter quite wealthy  this was just the beginning  one of the first things peter did after  selling paypal was starting clarium  capital clarium was a macro hedge fund  focused on trading currencies interest  rates commodities and equities and peter  based the firm's investment thesis  around the peak oil theory which states  that the world is running out of oil and  there are no easy alternatives this  strategy proved to be extremely  successful for peter as clarion posted  40 growth year after year he grew the  fund from just 10 million from himself  in 2002 to 7 billion in 2008 but by 2011  most investors left the fund not because  clarion got crushed by the financial  crisis but because peter missed out on  buying the dip this was quite  unfortunate but by this point peter's  initial 10 million dollars had grown to  230 million dollars so i think it worked  out just fine  aside from starting clarium peter also  founded palantir in may of 2003.  palantir is a data analytics company  that helps the government track down  criminals monitor supply chains and more  recently predict corporate outbreaks  volunteer has always been a popular  investment within the tech world but  since their ipo in 2020 volunteer has  become a popular choice amongst retail  investors as well  the company saw valuations as high as 80  billion dollars following their ipo  but they have since settled down at a  more modest 38 billion dollars  around the same time as starting  volunteer peter became an angel investor  in facebook in 2004 he invested 500 000  in exchange for 10.2 percent of the  company this gave him a spot on the  board and peter actually helped facebook  with future funding rounds but ever  since facebook went public peter has  been slowly selling off his stake he  still has a few thousand shares of  facebook left but compared to his 16.8  million shares when facebook went public  this is nothing  though peter gave up most of his shares  he did cash out over a billion dollars  securing a casual 2000x return and did i  mention facebook was just one of his  star performers  in 2005 peter created something called  the founders fund which was focused on  capitalizing on early stage companies  and their list of insane investments  literally goes on forever  we have airbnb slide.com linkedin  friendster rap leaf jenna.com yammer  yelp spotify powerset practice fusion  metamed vader spacex ironport wadizin  big thing cap linked quora nanotronics  imaging ripple transferwise stripe  block.one and old school  peter also purchased 15 to 20 million  dollars worth of bitcoin in mid-2017 and  he gave metallic butter in 100 000 to  drop out of college and found ethereum  like i said peter is literally involved  in everything and we still have more to  cover in 2012 peter founded valor  ventures which is focused on investing  in startups outside of silicon valley  that same year he also founded mitral  capital which is focused on investing in  mid-stage companies that are ready to  scale  on top of all of this peter became a  part-time partner at y combinator in  march of 2015. however this only lasted  two years as peter quickly formed some  beef with the other leaders and that  brings us into peter's beef with  everyone  we've already covered peter's beef with  stanford's super liberal views and his  hatred for inflation but this was just  scratching the surface peter has a  tendency of hitting institutions in  general and this makes sense given that  he's a libertarian one of his most  controversial views is his loathing of  higher education  of course peter isn't against education  or learning by any means but he strongly  believes that modern universities are  overpriced relics that are holding back  real innovation he thinks that people  should skip college that higher  education has brainwashed silicon valley  that higher education is the biggest  bubble in the world and that brilliant  kids don't need college one of his  efforts in fighting college has been the  teal fellowship which offers students  100 000 to drop out of school and start  a business ethereum was actually a  product of one of these fellowships  peter's beef isn't just limited to  institutions either he has plenty of  beef with the people as well with one of  his most notable beefs being with elon  musk as we touched on before paypal  merged with dex.com in the early 2000s  and elon would eventually become the ceo  of the joint company but elon had very  different views about the future of the  company and the branding  peter and the rest of the board didn't  really agree with this vision so they  would fire elon while he was on vacation  elon obviously felt that this was a  backstab move from peter and this is  where the beef really started peter  didn't mean for this to be personal and  both of them have tried to make amends  for example peter would invest 20  million dollars in spacex in 2008 and  help save the company from bankruptcy  also i'm sure they both have immense  respect for each other's skills and  capabilities  but with that being said they still  trash-talk each other all the time  peter has called elon a fraud and a  braggart and he thinks that elon isn't a  good role model  meanwhile elon thinks that peter is a  sociopath clearly peter and elon have a  love-hate relationship but not all of  peter's relationships are so benign some  of his relationships are just hate hate  relationships and the best example of  this is the gawker lawsuit in 2016 terry  boleya filed a lawsuit against gawker  media for invasion of privacy and  emotional distress  peter had nothing to do with the lawsuit  itself but he decided to fund terry with  the 10 million dollars to fight against  gawker media and this helped terry win a  140 million lawsuit against gawker which  forced the media company to shut down  now terry was in the right 